<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Arles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">314</span> · Also known as Synod of Arles (314)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> was a church council summoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> to Arles in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 314 to settle the Donatist dispute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. It ruled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a> and issued canons on church discipline. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> present were three from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and probably Lincoln, the earliest firm evidence of an organised church in Roman Britain.</p></div>
